corpus = """The human heart beats about one hundred thousand times each day, pushing blood through a network of vessels that would stretch nearly one hundred thousand kilometers if laid end to end. Doctors have studied this rhythm for centuries, listening for the small irregularities that hint at deeper problems.

A patient walks into the clinic complaining of fatigue. The nurse takes a history, checks blood pressure, and orders routine bloodwork. Sometimes the answer is simple: low iron, a virus, too little sleep. Other times, the symptoms point toward something that needs a specialist, a scan, a second opinion.

Medicine has always been a study of patterns. A cough that lingers past three weeks. A fever that spikes each evening but fades by morning. A rash that appears on both hands but never the feet. Physicians spend years learning to notice these patterns, to weigh them against thousands of similar cases they have seen before.

Artificial intelligence, at its core, is also a study of patterns, though it learns them differently. Instead of years of clinical rounds, a model studies thousands of labeled examples, adjusting its internal weights each time it gets an answer wrong. Given enough examples, it starts to notice the same regularities a trained eye would catch: the subtle shadow on an X-ray, the slight asymmetry in a scan, the pattern hidden in a stream of vital signs.

Neither replaces the other. A model trained on medical images can flag a suspicious region faster than any human, but it cannot sit with a frightened patient and explain what happens next. That conversation still belongs to the people in white coats, the ones who chose this work because they wanted to help, one patient at a time."""
